13. REAL DEVICE AND BROWSER INFORMATION

Varek’s main differentiator is its ability to use information from the current browser environment.

Implement a dedicated capability and diagnostics layer.

Detectable information

Where supported, collect:

* Browser family.
* Browser version or version hints.
* Operating system or platform hints.
* Device category.
* Browser language.
* Locale.
* Time zone.
* Screen resolution.
* Viewport dimensions.
* Device pixel ratio.
* Color depth.
* Touch support.
* Pointer capabilities.
* Hardware concurrency.
* Approximate device memory.
* Online/offline state.
* Limited network quality hints.
* Color scheme preference.
* Reduced-motion preference.
* Storage quota estimates.
* Supported browser APIs.
* Selected permission states when queryable.

Use standards-based APIs where possible.

Use feature detection, not assumptions.

User-agent Client Hints may be unavailable or restricted.

Device memory values may be rounded or unavailable.

Hardware concurrency is not a guaranteed measurement of physical CPU cores.

Online status does not guarantee that the internet is reachable.

Do not use invasive fingerprinting or covert identification methods.

Evidence classification

Every diagnostic field should indicate one of:

* Observed.
* Estimated.
* User-provided.
* Unavailable.
* Requires permission.

Only present values as confirmed when they are genuinely established.

For example:

“Your browser reports approximately 8 GB of device memory.”

Do not say:

“Your computer has exactly 8 GB of RAM.”

Unsupported information

A normal website cannot freely inspect:

* Installed native applications.
* All device files.
* Exact CPU or GPU utilization.
* Running system processes.
* Exact physical memory usage.
* Hardware temperatures.
* Battery health.
* Complete browsing history.
* Arbitrary browser settings.
* Other applications’ private data.

Do not implement fake values for these fields.

If the user asks for inaccessible information, explain what cannot be read and how they can check it manually.

Where safe, ask the user to provide relevant information so Zulvyn can continue troubleshooting.

Diagnostics dashboard

Provide a minimal, optional diagnostic view.

It should feel like a refined utility panel, not a colorful analytics dashboard.

Group information by:

* Browser.
* Device.
* Display.
* Network.
* Storage.
* Permissions.
* Capabilities.

Allow users to copy individual results or export a diagnostic report.

Provide a privacy-safe preview of data before export.

Do not automatically upload the report anywhere.

