Contextual page capabilities

With an explicit user action and appropriate permission, support:

* Current page title.
* Current page URL.
* Domain.
* Basic document metadata.
* Basic page structure statistics.
* Relevant technical attributes.
* Limited troubleshooting observations.
* Permissioned extraction of page text for simple local analysis.

Do not assume the extension can understand arbitrary page content like an actual LLM.

If providing page summaries, use clearly defined extractive techniques and disclose their limitations.

Avoid unsupported claims about the page’s meaning.

Do not read sensitive page contents without deliberate user action.

Permissions

Use the minimum permissions necessary.

Possible permissions include:

* storage
* sidePanel
* activeTab
* scripting

Introduce additional permissions only for concrete implemented features.

Avoid blanket access to all websites.

Prefer temporary activeTab access or optional site permissions.

Do not automatically inspect every website the user visits.

Web-to-extension integration

The website should work independently without the extension.

If a supported Chrome extension is installed, the website may offer additional capabilities through a secure, explicit bridge.

Use established extension messaging mechanisms.

For any externally connectable bridge:

* Restrict allowed website origins.
* Validate message types and payloads.
* Reject unauthorized senders.
* Require user consent for sensitive operations.
* Never treat a web page request as unrestricted permission to access browser data.

Do not assume the extension is installed.

Do not require the extension for basic Zulvyn functionality.

Extension packaging

The extension must contain its own bundled executable code.

Do not load remotely hosted executable JavaScript from GitHub Pages.

